{
  "schema": "la-module@1",
  "module_key": "2024-10-20-liaison-help",
  "kind": "guide",
  "status": "written",
  "title": "French Flow · Liaison",
  "description": "Learn how French words connect smoothly",
  "can_do": [
    "Hear when a silent letter links to the next word, as in {les amis}.",
    "Tell {ils sont} (they are) from {ils ont} (they have) by ear."
  ],
  "minutes": 4,
  "line": "How a silent letter links to the next word, and why it changes what you hear.",
  "parts": [
    {
      "id": "p-silent-letters-at-the",
      "heading": "Silent letters at the end",
      "text": [
        "Most consonants at the end of a French word are silent. You write them, but you don't say them."
      ],
      "note": "Final s, t, x and d are usually silent. The plural s is silent too, so {ami} and {amis} sound the same. A final r or l is often heard, as in {bonjour} and {il}.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "pairs": [
            [
              "les",
              "lay"
            ],
            [
              "vous",
              "voo"
            ],
            [
              "chat",
              "sha"
            ],
            [
              "salut",
              "sa-loo"
            ],
            [
              "enfant",
              "ahn-fahn"
            ]
          ],
          "heading": "Silent at the end"
        },
        {
          "pairs": [
            [
              "bonjour",
              "bon-zhoor"
            ],
            [
              "bonsoir",
              "bon-swahr"
            ],
            [
              "il",
              "eel"
            ]
          ],
          "heading": "Heard at the end"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-words-that-run-together",
      "heading": "Words that run together",
      "text": [
        "Many French words end in a letter you do not say. When the next word starts with a vowel sound (a, e, i, o, u or a silent h), that letter wakes up and links the two words. This link is called liaison."
      ],
      "note": "The silent s at the end of {les}, {vous} and {ils} turns into a z sound before a vowel.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "ties": [
            {
              "fr": "les amis",
              "a": "les",
              "b": "amis",
              "link": "s",
              "sound": "lay [z]a-mee",
              "en": "the friends"
            },
            {
              "fr": "vous êtes",
              "a": "vous",
              "b": "êtes",
              "link": "s",
              "sound": "voo [z]et",
              "en": "you are"
            },
            {
              "fr": "ils ont",
              "a": "ils",
              "b": "ont",
              "link": "s",
              "sound": "eel [z]on",
              "en": "they have"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-with-and-without",
      "heading": "With and without",
      "text": [
        "Hear each pair without the link, then with it. Without it, the words blur into one. With it, you can hear each word."
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "cmp": {
            "left": "Without the link",
            "right": "With the link",
            "rows": [
              [
                {
                  "fr": "vous avez",
                  "say": "vou avez"
                },
                {
                  "fr": "vous avez",
                  "say": "vous avez"
                }
              ],
              [
                {
                  "fr": "les hommes",
                  "say": "leh hommes"
                },
                {
                  "fr": "les hommes",
                  "say": "les hommes"
                }
              ]
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-a-link-can-change",
      "heading": "A link can change the meaning",
      "text": [
        "{ils sont} means they are. {ils ont} means they have. The only sound that tells them apart is s or z."
      ],
      "note": "In {ils sont}, the s sound belongs to {sont}. In {ils ont}, the z sound is carried over from the end of {ils}.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "cmp": {
            "left": "No link",
            "right": "A link",
            "rows": [
              [
                {
                  "fr": "ils sont",
                  "say": "ils sont",
                  "en": "they are",
                  "sub": "{sont} starts with s"
                },
                {
                  "fr": "ils ont",
                  "say": "ils ont",
                  "en": "they have",
                  "sub": "{ont} starts with a vowel"
                }
              ]
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-more-to-hear",
      "heading": "More to hear",
      "text": [
        "The same link, in words you know."
      ],
      "note": "You do not need to work it out each time. Copy what you hear, and the links come with practice.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "ties": [
            {
              "fr": "elles ont",
              "a": "elles",
              "b": "ont",
              "link": "s",
              "sound": "el [z]on",
              "en": "they have (feminine)"
            },
            {
              "fr": "nous avons",
              "a": "nous",
              "b": "avons",
              "link": "s",
              "sound": "noo [z]a-von",
              "en": "we have"
            },
            {
              "fr": "les enfants",
              "a": "les",
              "b": "enfants",
              "link": "s",
              "sound": "lay [z]on-fon",
              "en": "the children"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-elision-l-ami-j",
      "heading": "Elision: {l'ami, j'ai}",
      "text": [
        "Before a vowel sound, {le}, {la} and {je} drop their vowel and take an apostrophe. The two words then sound like one."
      ],
      "note": "A silent h counts as a vowel, so {homme} takes {l'} as well. The same happens to {si} before {il} in {s'il vous plaît}. Elision and liaison work together. One friend is {l'ami}. More than one is {les amis}, where {les} keeps its s and links with a z sound.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "pairs": [
            [
              "l'ami",
              "le + ami"
            ],
            [
              "l'amie",
              "la + amie"
            ],
            [
              "l'enfant",
              "le + enfant"
            ],
            [
              "l'homme",
              "le + homme"
            ],
            [
              "j'ai",
              "je + ai"
            ],
            [
              "s'il vous plaît",
              "si + il"
            ]
          ],
          "heading": "Words You Know"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "checks": [
    {
      "id": "k1",
      "ask": "Listen. Do the words link with a z sound?",
      "say": "les enfants",
      "show": "les enfants",
      "sound": "lay zon-fon",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Yes, a z",
          "right": true
        },
        {
          "text": "No"
        }
      ],
      "why": "{enfants} starts with a vowel, so the s of {les} sounds as z."
    },
    {
      "id": "k2",
      "ask": "Listen. Do you hear a z between the words?",
      "say": "ils sont",
      "show": "ils sont",
      "sound": "eel son",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Yes, a z"
        },
        {
          "text": "No, an s",
          "right": true
        }
      ],
      "why": "{sont} starts with s, so nothing links. The s you hear starts {sont}."
    },
    {
      "id": "k3",
      "ask": "Listen. Which one did you hear?",
      "say": "ils ont",
      "sound": "eel zon",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "ils sont, they are",
          "fr": true
        },
        {
          "text": "ils ont, they have",
          "fr": true,
          "right": true
        }
      ],
      "why": "The z links {ils} to {ont}: {ils ont}, they have."
    }
  ]
}
